  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances are expected\nthrough early next week.\n\nA deep southerly flow will continue around high pressure over \nthe Atlantic, ushering plenty of moisture. PWATS will be near or\ngreater than 2\" in the valleys with 1-2\" in the mountains, \nwhich is about 1- 2 SD above climo. A surface trough and \nshortwave energy will trigger scattered showers/thunderstorms, \nbut it should not be a washout this afternoon/evening. The main\nthreat will be brief heavy downpours and gusty winds. However, \nshear is on the lower side, so the probability of severe storms\nis lower. If some storms train over the same area, localized \nflash flooding can occur. The pattern does favor the possibility\nfor training convection (cloud layer winds nearly parallel to \nlow-level wind), but the coverage should be isolated. \n\nA similar pattern will hold on for Thursday and Friday while the \nsubtropical jet strengthens to the north and west. Therefore, more \nshowers and thunderstorms are likely along with the threat for \nisolated instances of flash flooding and locally damaging wind \ngusts. Hotter conditions may cause relatively steeper low-level \nlapse rates and the wind field strengthens a bit (especially \nFriday). \n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft will develop for the weekend into next \nweek, bringing more seasonably hot and humid conditions along with \ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms.",
